---

<section id="hardware" class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">the hardware</p>
    <div class="quiz-head">
      <h2>The best first Linux machine is one you already own.</h2>
      <p>
        A laptop aging in a closet is already a Linux computer; nobody has told
        it yet. No spare machine, or your only computer is a MacBook staying on
        macOS? Then you are shopping, and this is the first era in which an
        entire industry builds for Linux on purpose. One rule if you buy:
        prefer AMD or Intel graphics unless you specifically need CUDA. It
        removes the biggest remaining source of Linux friction before you even
        start.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="vendors">
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>already owned</h3>
        <p>
          Almost any laptop from the past decade. The community classic: a used
          ThinkPad, built like a tank, priced like a phone case. Stranded on
          Windows 10? <a href="https://endof10.org/">End of 10</a> runs
          real-world install events for exactly your machine.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>linux-first builders</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://frame.work">Framework</a> (modular, repair it
          yourself; the new Laptop 13 Pro ships prebuilt with Linux),
          <a href="https://www.tuxedocomputers.com">Tuxedo</a>,
          <a href="https://system76.com">System76</a>,
          <a href="https://slimbook.com">Slimbook</a>,
          <a href="https://starlabs.systems">Star Labs</a>. Machines designed
          for Linux, not merely tolerating it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>big brands, official support</h3>
        <p>
          Lenovo and Dell sell Linux-certified configurations with the penguin's
          blessing. Corporate, boring, works.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>small and silent</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://www.bee-link.com">Beelink</a> and
          <a href="https://www.minisforum.com">Minisforum</a> mini PCs: a full
          desktop the size of a sandwich, happy to run anything.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>the gateway drug</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/steamdeck">Steam Deck</a>. If
          you own one, you have been running Linux all along; you just called
          it gaming.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vendor">
        <h3>local-AI workstations</h3>
        <p>
          The new unified-memory AMD boxes (Framework Desktop, Beelink's AI Max
          line) hold large models in RAM and run them at your desk, no cloud
          involved. Your permanently available sysadmin, self-hosted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="quiz-note">
      No affiliate links; if that ever changes it will be
      disclosed in letters this size. The real quiz will ask whether you need
      hardware at all, and fold the answer into your blueprint.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
